WEBVTT

1
00:00:05.801 --> 00:00:13.125
Hi, Little Fox readers!

2
00:00:13.945 --> 00:00:16.587
We've been learning about the environment, so Mr.

3
00:00:16.647 --> 00:00:22.070
Fay told us about a landmark that goes perfectly with this topic—Easter Island.

4
00:00:22.991 --> 00:00:28.894
Also known as Rapa Nui, this island in the South Pacific is the most remote inhabited island in

5
00:00:28.934 --> 00:00:29.494
the world.

6
00:00:30.515 --> 00:00:36.607
And it's famous for something else: hundreds of huge, mysterious statues called moai.

7
00:00:37.527 --> 00:00:42.811
At first I couldn't imagine how a tiny island with a bunch of statues is connected to taking

8
00:00:42.871 --> 00:00:44.111
care of the environment.

9
00:00:44.992 --> 00:00:49.515
But I found out that these statues have a lot to do with the environment.

10
00:00:50.335 --> 00:00:51.476
I'll get to that in a minute.

11
00:00:52.417 --> 00:00:57.120
First let me tell you about the island itself—it has a really interesting history!

12
00:00:58.620 --> 00:01:03.584
Easter Island was first settled by Polynesians sometime around 400 AD.

13
00:01:04.385 --> 00:01:11.470
The island's landscape is very sparse now, but when those settlers, now called the Rapanui, first arrived in

14
00:01:11.510 --> 00:01:14.773
their canoes, the whole island was covered in palm trees.

15
00:01:15.814 --> 00:01:18.576
Nobody is sure why these people came to the island.

16
00:01:19.597 --> 00:01:25.742
I'm amazed that they even found it because it's small—only 163 square kilometers.

17
00:01:26.743 --> 00:01:32.587
And it's in the middle of nowhere, almost 2,000 kilometers from Pitcairn Island, the nearest land.

18
00:01:33.547 --> 00:01:39.351
Over time the Rapanui changed the island's landscape by cutting down trees and clearing the land to plant

19
00:01:39.411 --> 00:01:49.818
crops. And between 1000 and 1680 AD, they transformed the landscape in another dramatic way—by sculpting hundreds of

20
00:01:49.958 --> 00:01:52.820
moai and placing them all over the island.

21
00:01:54.281 --> 00:01:58.404
The Rapanui carved the moai out of yellowish-gray volcanic rock.

22
00:01:59.304 --> 00:02:05.971
The heads look a little creepy but cool too, with long noses and ears, and protruding foreheads above

23
00:02:06.091 --> 00:02:07.092
deep-set eyes.

24
00:02:08.073 --> 00:02:12.977
Archaeologists believe these statues depict important Rapanui ancestors and chiefs.

25
00:02:14.058 --> 00:02:17.842
Some of the moai also have round topknots on top of their heads.

26
00:02:18.863 --> 00:02:23.848
The topknots look like big red hats because they're made from red volcanic rock.

27
00:02:24.808 --> 00:02:29.512
Most of the moai were carved out of the bedrock of the volcano Rano Raraku.

28
00:02:30.933 --> 00:02:34.156
First, sculptors shaped the statues' faces and heads.

29
00:02:34.997 --> 00:02:38.840
When that was done, the statues were cut free from the surrounding rock.

30
00:02:39.761 --> 00:02:45.846
Next, they were lowered down the slope of the volcano and propped up vertically in shallow holes so

31
00:02:45.866 --> 00:02:47.167
the backs could be carved.

32
00:02:48.108 --> 00:02:50.109
Today there are 397 moai remaining at Rano Raraku.

33
00:02:54.028 --> 00:02:59.294
Some are still attached to the bedrock, including one that's over 20 meters long!

34
00:03:00.255 --> 00:03:02.438
Others are standing up in those shallow holes.

35
00:03:03.379 --> 00:03:09.646
But because of silt running down the slope of the volcano over so many years, they're partially buried.

36
00:03:10.987 --> 00:03:14.932
Completed moai had to be moved to various villages around the island.

37
00:03:15.836 --> 00:03:22.058
This must have been extremely difficult since the average moai was over 4 meters tall and weighed over

38
00:03:22.538 --> 00:03:23.758
12 metric tons.

39
00:03:24.718 --> 00:03:29.520
Moving one probably required between 50 and 150 people!

40
00:03:30.420 --> 00:03:34.001
Nobody knows for sure exactly how the moai were moved.

41
00:03:34.901 --> 00:03:40.903
Many people believe they were placed on wooden sleds that were towed with ropes, while others believe that

42
00:03:40.923 --> 00:03:45.024
the statues were tilted slightly and then "walked" across the island.

43
00:03:46.020 --> 00:03:47.982
My favorite theory is one that Mr.

44
00:03:48.062 --> 00:03:54.788
Fay told us, from an island legend: The moai got up and walked by themselves!

45
00:03:56.390 --> 00:04:00.273
Once moved, moai were placed on rectangular stone platforms.

46
00:04:01.254 --> 00:04:05.018
Most of these platforms were placed near the coast, overlooking villages.

47
00:04:05.999 --> 00:04:11.564
Each one had between one and fifteen moai standing in a row, with their backs to the sea.

48
00:04:12.492 --> 00:04:16.315
These moai were given eyes made from shells and polished stones.

49
00:04:17.235 --> 00:04:25.881
The Rapanui had no contact with other people until Easter Sunday, 1722, when a Dutch explorer named Jacob

50
00:04:25.941 --> 00:04:30.204
Roggeveen arrived on the island and started calling it Easter Island.

51
00:04:31.165 --> 00:04:34.487
Roggeveen wrote that the islanders worshipped the moai.

52
00:04:35.308 --> 00:04:42.857
He also wrote that, unlike most Polynesian islands, Easter Island had almost no trees, just small shrubs and

53
00:04:42.897 --> 00:04:52.182
grass. Sometime after Roggeveen's arrival, a civil war broke out on the island, probably because of overpopulation and

54
00:04:52.242 --> 00:04:53.483
limited resources.

55
00:04:54.364 --> 00:04:59.487
The Rapanui stopped making moai and even started knocking each others' down!

56
00:05:00.407 --> 00:05:04.370
Today Easter Island is part of Rapa Nui National Park.

57
00:05:05.290 --> 00:05:07.952
Visitors can get there from Santiago, Chile.

58
00:05:08.840 --> 00:05:15.666
Almost everyone on the island lives in Hanga Roa, the island's only town, which has some hotels, restaurants,

59
00:05:15.867 --> 00:05:16.487
and shops.

60
00:05:17.408 --> 00:05:23.574
Mr. Fay said that there are about nine hundred moai still scattered all over the island, so it's

61
00:05:23.614 --> 00:05:26.536
a good idea to hire a guide who can show you around.

62
00:05:27.617 --> 00:05:34.083
One very popular site for tourists is the quarry at Rano Raraku, where you can see the unfinished

63
00:05:34.203 --> 00:05:39.437
moai. I bet you're still wondering what Easter Island has to do with the environment.

64
00:05:40.337 --> 00:05:47.619
Well, many experts believe the Rapanui continued cutting down trees to use as firewood and building materials for

65
00:05:47.659 --> 00:05:48.359
many years.

66
00:05:49.240 --> 00:05:52.341
They also used them as tools for moving the giant moai.

67
00:05:53.161 --> 00:05:58.142
Eventually every single tree on the entire island was gone!

68
00:05:59.002 --> 00:06:04.704
Without trees the soil suffered from severe erosion and became depleted of its nutrients.

69
00:06:05.656 --> 00:06:07.577
Farming the land became difficult.

70
00:06:08.437 --> 00:06:09.157
I asked Mr.

71
00:06:09.217 --> 00:06:11.658
Fay why the Rapanui didn't leave.

72
00:06:12.538 --> 00:06:14.279
He said they probably couldn't.

73
00:06:15.239 --> 00:06:17.079
To leave, they would have needed canoes.

74
00:06:18.000 --> 00:06:19.560
And what do you need to make canoes?

75
00:06:20.520 --> 00:06:22.741
Yep, you got it—trees!

76
00:06:24.262 --> 00:06:29.403
The story of Easter Island shows us just how important it is to take care of the environment

77
00:06:29.983 --> 00:06:32.284
and use natural resources wisely.

78
00:06:33.212 --> 00:06:37.373
I was really glad to learn that new trees are now being planted on Easter Island.

79
00:06:38.253 --> 00:06:44.735
Maybe someday palm trees will cover the island again, just as they did when the Rapanui first arrived.

80
00:06:45.615 --> 00:06:49.716
After our landmarks lesson, we got to make our own moai out of clay.

81
00:06:50.537 --> 00:06:52.677
I made mine standing on a platform.

82
00:06:53.498 --> 00:07:00.419
It looked a little sad all by itself, so I added something else: a tall, leafy palm tree!

83
00:07:01.380 --> 00:07:02.400
See you next week!
